  "slug": "embedding-kernels-function-relationships-30bc",
  "summary": "Recent preprints apply long-read sequencing and barcoded tracing to genome assembly accuracy, mutation rates, and neuronal projections.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the use of high-accuracy sequencing and barcoding to quantify genomic and cellular variation across taxa. Cortical neuron point mutation rates estimated with PacBio HiFi sequencing reach about 25.9 mutations per diploid genome per year, similar in mice and humans. A separate preprint models equilibrium trinucleotide genome composition from context-dependent mutation spectra in 25 plant and 10 mammalian species, incorporating GC-biased gene conversion. A sequencing-based barcoded connectomics method maps single-neuron projections rapidly across seven vertebrate species, from frogs to primates. Meanwhile, a critique argues that the 'near T2T' Canton-S Drosophila assembly is inconsistent with three other independent Canton-S assemblies, raising questions about reference accuracy. The new elements are the cross-species scale of mutation and composition modelling and the barcoded projection mapping, which together extend sequencing from reference assembly to neuronal and mutational inference.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
